    "abstract": "Urban innovation agendas increasingly organise public investment around visible digital and development initiatives, while maintenance-related process innovation receives uneven institutional recognition. This paper defines the urban innovation paradox as a self-undermining condition in which cities pursue efficiency and resilience through innovation while giving limited policy visibility to the maintenance and renewal capabilities on which digital systems depend. Drawing on process-innovation research, the attention-based view, and infrastructure governance, I identify four pathways through which the paradox may arise: programme eligibility, political visibility, organisational attention, and lifecycle responsibility. Evidence from maintenance technologies and smart-city implementation illustrates both the innovative content of maintenance and the governance conditions affecting its uptake. Two heuristic frameworks distinguish institutional recognition from innovation type and relate policy scope to infrastructure condition. The analysis proposes broader programme eligibility and stronger lifecycle coupling, supported by outcome-based evaluation and clearer responsibility, while outlining comparative and sectoral tests for future research and policy evaluation.",
