  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The paper relies on conceptual frameworks and illustrative examples of maintenance technologies rather than a systematic empirical dataset.",
      "what": "This paper conceptualizes the 'urban innovation paradox,' a condition where cities invest in highly visible digital initiatives while neglecting the essential maintenance and renewal capabilities required to sustain them. It identifies four specific pathways driving this paradox—programme eligibility, political visibility, organisational attention, and lifecycle responsibility—and introduces two heuristic frameworks that map institutional recognition against innovation types and policy scope against infrastructure conditions.",
      "why": "It establishes a theoretical foundation for recognizing maintenance as a form of process innovation, shifting the urban policy focus from merely deploying new smart technologies to managing their long-term survival and lifecycle costs."
    }
